Negative control for the ONE law the controlled table rests on: the module owns
the sort model, and this surface only lifts intent to it.

Makes the renderer own sorting instead. `state.sorting` reads a local ref the
component mutates, and the header click updates that ref rather than emitting a
`TableIntent` through `channel.emit`. The table still LOOKS right — the header
toggles, the chevron flips, the arrow points the way the user clicked — while
the composable is never told, the query is never re-run, and the rows underneath
never move. Precisely the shape a screenshot cannot tell from a working sort.

Its target is the sort path restored on 2026-08-11: before that the columns
carried no sort declaration at all, `getCanSort()` was false for every header,
and this mutant was DEAD — nothing it changes could be reached. It is live again
because a declared-sortable column now renders a real control.

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`.

Reds (component lane, measured 2026-08-11 — 1 failed, 67 passed, plus the
pre-existing `list-surface-states.spec.ts` row-list case this mutant does not
touch):
  list-surface.spec.ts   "emits a sort intent naming the DECLARED column, not a
                           row key"

Everything else stays GREEN, including "renders its sort/pagination display from
channel.read(), never from an owned state field" — that case measures the
PAGINATION display, so the sort half of its own title is unguarded. A suite that
graded this mutant a pass on the strength of that title would be measuring its
own wording.

